/P 390 0 R
/T (1.1 Background of the Project)
/E (1.1 Background of the Project)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
626 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 390 0 R
/E (Residential societies are complex communities in which a large number of people, properties, services, financial activities, and administrative operations have to be managed on a continuous basis. A residential society is not limited to the physical management of flats and buildings; it also involves maintaining resident information, monitoring visitors, coordinating security and housekeeping staff, generating maintenance bills, recording payments, handling complaints, publishing notices, managing common facilities, and maintaining amenity booking schedules. As the size of a residential community increases, the volume of information generated through these activities also increases. Managing such information manually through notebooks, paper registers, spreadsheets, or separate communication platforms can become difficult because the information is distributed across different locations and may not always remain synchronized. The Society Management Portal, also referred to in the project design as Haven Society Management, has been designed as a centralized residential community management system to organize these activities within a structured digital environment. The project design specifically defines the system as a residential society operations and community management system covering flats, residents, visitors, staff, maintenance billing, payments, complaints, notices, amenities, and amenity bookings.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
627 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 390 0 R
/E (The fundamental idea behind the project is to create a single application through which important society operations can be represented, accessed, and managed in an organized manner. In a conventional society management environment, information about a flat may be stored separately from information about its residents, while visitor records may be maintained by security personnel and maintenance information may be handled by the finance or administrative team. Complaints may be received through informal communication channels, and amenity reservations may be maintained using separate registers or spreadsheets. Such an arrangement makes it difficult to obtain a complete and consistent view of society activities. The proposed system addresses this issue by organizing the major operational areas into clearly defined modules and connecting related records through a relational data model. For example, a resident can be associated with a particular flat, a visitor can be associated with a destination flat, a maintenance bill can be associated with a flat, a payment can be associated with both a bill and a resident, and an amenity booking can be associated with both a resident and an amenity. The project therefore treats society management not as a collection of independent functions but as an interconnected information system in which changes and records in one area can have relevance to another.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
628 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 390 0 R
/E (The project has been designed using a modern web application approach. The frontend is developed using React and Vite, with HTML and CSS forming the basic web presentation technologies. React)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
629 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 390 0 R
/E (Page 8)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
