>>
endobj
631 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 391 0 R
/T (provides the component-based structure required to develop reusable user-interface elements, while Vite provides the development and build environment. The project design also identifies React Router, Recharts, and Lucide React as application libraries. The intended database is MySQL, with the database named society_management. The architecture places a context and service layer between the React interface and the future backend API, thereby avoiding direct communication between the frontend and the database. This separation is important from a software engineering perspective because it allows the presentation layer, application logic, and data storage responsibilities to remain conceptually distinct.)
/E (provides the component-based structure required to develop reusable user-interface elements, while Vite provides the development and build environment. The project design also identifies React Router, Recharts, and Lucide React as application libraries. The intended database is MySQL, with the database named society_management. The architecture places a context and service layer between the React interface and the future backend API, thereby avoiding direct communication between the frontend and the database. This separation is important from a software engineering perspective because it allows the presentation layer, application logic, and data storage responsibilities to remain conceptually distinct.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
632 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 391 0 R
/T (The system is also designed around different categories of users because the responsibilities of a residential society are distributed among different people. An administrator may require broad access to society records, whereas a treasurer primarily deals with financial information. A secretary may work with residents, visitors, complaints, notices, amenities, and bookings, while security personnel require access mainly to visitor-related operations. Residents require access to services relevant to their own participation in society activities. This role-oriented approach is important because a common management system should not assume that every user needs access to every operation. Instead, the interface can present functionality according to the responsibilities associated with a particular role. The project design identifies Resident, Staff, Committee, and Administrator as major user categories within the overall system flow, while the implemented frontend further organizes access according to eight specific operational roles.)
/E (The system is also designed around different categories of users because the responsibilities of a residential society are distributed among different people. An administrator may require broad access to society records, whereas a treasurer primarily deals with financial information. A secretary may work with residents, visitors, complaints, notices, amenities, and bookings, while security personnel require access mainly to visitor-related operations. Residents require access to services relevant to their own participation in society activities. This role-oriented approach is important because a common management system should not assume that every user needs access to every operation. Instead, the interface can present functionality according to the responsibilities associated with a particular role. The project design identifies Resident, Staff, Committee, and Administrator as major user categories within the overall system flow, while the implemented frontend further organizes access according to eight specific operational roles.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
633 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 391 0 R
/T (Another important aspect of the project is the use of a relational database design. The system contains ten major entities: flats, residents, visitors, staff, maintenance_bills, payments, complaints, notices, amenities, and amenity_bookings. These entities represent different areas of society management while maintaining relationships wherever information is naturally connected. Such a design provides a structured foundation for maintaining consistency and reducing unnecessary duplication of information.)
/E (Another important aspect of the project is the use of a relational database design. The system contains ten major entities: flats, residents, visitors, staff, maintenance_bills, payments, complaints, notices, amenities, and amenity_bookings. These entities represent different areas of society management while maintaining relationships wherever information is naturally connected. Such a design provides a structured foundation for maintaining consistency and reducing unnecessary duplication of information.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
634 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 391 0 R
/T (The project is therefore intended not merely as a collection of web pages but as a structured software system demonstrating how residential community operations can be modeled using modern web technologies and relational database principles. The frontend provides the visible interaction layer, while the service architecture establishes a foundation for future backend integration. The project design explicitly identifies the backend API as an integration point between the React and Vite application and the MySQL society_management database. This architectural decision is important because a production-ready application should not allow a browser-based frontend to directly access the database. Instead, requests should pass through an appropriate server-side API where authentication, authorization, validation, business rules, and database operations can be controlled.)
/E (The project is therefore intended not merely as a collection of web pages but as a structured software system demonstrating how residential community operations can be modeled using modern web technologies and relational database principles. The frontend provides the visible interaction layer, while the service architecture establishes a foundation for future backend integration. The project design explicitly identifies the backend API as an integration point between the React and Vite application and the MySQL society_management database. This architectural decision is important because a production-ready application should not allow a browser-based frontend to directly access the database. Instead, requests should pass through an appropriate server-side API where authentication, authorization, validation, business rules, and database operations can be controlled.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
