637 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 392 0 R
/E (Most small and medium residential societies in India still run their daily operations through a mixture of paper registers, spreadsheets, and messaging groups. The security guard writes visitor entries into a notebook at the gate, the treasurer tracks maintenance dues in a spreadsheet that only one person can edit, complaints arrive as phone calls or WhatsApp messages, and notices are pasted on a board in the lobby or forwarded through chat groups where they are quickly buried under other messages. Each of these practices works in isolation, but together they create a fragmented picture of the society in which no single person can easily answer basic questions such as "Which flats have unpaid bills this month?", "Who is currently inside the premises?", or "How many complaints are still open?")
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
638 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 392 0 R
/E (This fragmentation produces several concrete problems. First, information is duplicated and inconsistent: the same resident's phone number may exist in three different places, and when it changes only one copy is updated. Second, financial tracking is slow and error-prone: matching payments to bills manually takes time, and residents often have to ask the treasurer to confirm whether their payment was recorded. Third, accountability is weak: complaints communicated verbally have no record of when they were raised, who is handling them, or whether they were resolved. Fourth, gate security depends on handwriting: visitor registers are difficult to search, are easily lost, and cannot tell a resident in real time that their guest has arrived. Fifth, shared amenities are double-booked because there is no single schedule that everyone can see.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
639 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 392 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
640 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 392 0 R
/E (The Society Management Portal therefore represents an attempt to bring several common residential society activities into one organized digital platform. The system focuses on the practical requirements of day-to-day society administration while also demonstrating important concepts in software development, including modular architecture, reusable components, relational database design, role-based access, CRUD operations, state management, routing, user-interface design, and future API integration. Its ten-table relational structure separates property, people, finance, communication, service, and amenity concerns while preserving relationships through foreign keys and exposing those areas through a responsive role-based interface.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
