/T (Security note. In the current frontend-only release, these checks control what the interface displays. They are not a substitute for server-side security, because a determined user can modify code running in their own browser. When the backend is connected, the same rules will be enforced in Express middleware on every request, passwords will be stored as bcrypt hashes instead of plain text, and the session will be a signed JWT rather than a user object in localStorage.)
/E (Security note. In the current frontend-only release, these checks control what the interface displays. They are not a substitute for server-side security, because a determined user can modify code running in their own browser. When the backend is connected, the same rules will be enforced in Express middleware on every request, passwords will be stored as bcrypt hashes instead of plain text, and the session will be a signed JWT rather than a user object in localStorage.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [79]
