<<
/Title (1.1 Background of the Project)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9349 0 R
>>
endobj
9354 0 obj
<<
/Title (provides the component-based structure required to develop reusable user-interface elements, while Vite provides the development and build environment. The project design also identifies React Router, Recharts, and Lucide React as application libraries. The intended database is MySQL, with the database named society_management. The architecture places a context and service layer between the React interface and the future backend API, thereby avoiding direct communication between the frontend and the database. This separation is important from a software engineering perspective because it allows the presentation layer, application logic, and data storage responsibilities to remain conceptually distinct.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 9349 0 R
/Next 9356 0 R
>>
endobj
9355 0 obj
<<
/Title <422E34204465636C61726174697665206D6F64756C6520636F6E66696775726174696F6E2085207372632F636F6E6669672F7265736F75726365732E6A7320286578636572707429>
/Dest [88 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 9357 0 R
/First 9358 0 R
/Last 9358 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 9350 0 R
>>
endobj
9356 0 obj
<<
/Title (The system is also designed around different categories of users because the responsibilities of a residential society are distributed among different people. An administrator may require broad access to society records, whereas a treasurer primarily deals with financial information. A secretary may work with residents, visitors, complaints, notices, amenities, and bookings, while security personnel require access mainly to visitor-related operations. Residents require access to services relevant to their own participation in society activities. This role-oriented approach is important because a common management system should not assume that every user needs access to every operation. Instead, the interface can present functionality according to the responsibilities associated with a particular role. The project design identifies Resident, Staff, Committee, and Administrator as major user categories within the overall system flow, while the implemented frontend further organizes access according to eight specific operational roles.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 9354 0 R
/Next 9359 0 R
>>
endobj
9357 0 obj
<<
/Title <422E3320526F6C65207065726D697373696F6E732085207372632F636F6E6669672F7065726D697373696F6E732E6A7320286578636572707429>
/Dest [88 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 9360 0 R
/Next 9355 0 R
>>
endobj
9358 0 obj
<<
/Title (Page 79)
/Dest [88 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9355 0 R
>>
endobj
9359 0 obj
<<
/Title (Another important aspect of the project is the use of a relational database design. The system contains ten major entities: flats, residents, visitors, staff, maintenance_bills, payments, complaints, notices, amenities, and amenity_bookings. These entities represent different areas of society management while maintaining relationships wherever information is naturally connected. Such a design provides a structured foundation for maintaining consistency and reducing unnecessary duplication of information.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 9356 0 R
/Next 9361 0 R
>>
endobj
9360 0 obj
<<
/Title <422E3220526F7574652067756172642085207372632F636F6D706F6E656E74732F50726F746563746564526F7574652E6A7378>
/Dest [87 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 9362 0 R
/First 9363 0 R
/Last 9363 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 9357 0 R
>>
endobj
9361 0 obj
<<
/Title (The project is therefore intended not merely as a collection of web pages but as a structured software system demonstrating how residential community operations can be modeled using modern web technologies and relational database principles. The frontend provides the visible interaction layer, while the service architecture establishes a foundation for future backend integration. The project design explicitly identifies the backend API as an integration point between the React and Vite application and the MySQL society_management database. This architectural decision is important because a production-ready application should not allow a browser-based frontend to directly access the database. Instead, requests should pass through an appropriate server-side API where authentication, authorization, validation, business rules, and database operations can be controlled.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 9359 0 R
/Next 9364 0 R
>>
endobj
