/Title (The back end is the layer that will receive requests from the React application, apply business rules and security checks, and read from or write to the MySQL database. In the current release, the back end is represented by the service layer \(src/services/api.js\), which exposes asynchronous list, add, update, and remove functions for every resource and simulates network latency of 180 milliseconds. This allowed the complete user interface to be built and tested before the server was written.)
/Dest [29 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9461 0 R
/Next 9467 0 R
>>
endobj
9464 0 obj
<<
/Title (The planned back end follows a layered structure: routes define the URL endpoints; middleware verifies the JSON Web Token, checks the user's role against the same permission rules used in the frontend, and validates the request body; controllers coordinate the request; and repositories execute parameterized SQL statements. Every permission that is currently enforced in the browser will be enforced again on the server, because hiding a button in the interface is a usability measure, not a security control.)
/Dest [29 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9461 0 R
/Prev 9468 0 R
>>
endobj
