<<
/Title (Frontend hosting. The output of npm run build is a static dist/ folder containing index.html, one hashed JavaScript file, and one hashed CSS file. This folder can be served by any static host or content delivery network. Vercel and Netlify are the recommended platforms because they connect directly to the GitHub repository, build automatically on every push to main, provide free HTTPS certificates, and create preview deployments for pull requests. Because the application uses client- side routing, the host is configured to rewrite all paths to index.html, so that refreshing a page such as /payments does not return a 404 error.)
/Dest [35 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9535 0 R
/Prev 9547 0 R
/Next 9555 0 R
>>
endobj
9555 0 obj
<<
/Title (Backend and database hosting \(planned\). The Express API will be deployed to a Node.js hosting service such as Render, Railway, or an AWS EC2 instance, and the MySQL database to a managed service such as AWS RDS, Railway MySQL, or Aiven. The frontend will read the API base URL from an environment variable \(VITE_API_URL\), and secrets such as the database password and JWT signing key will be stored as environment variables on the server, never in the repository.)
/Dest [35 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9535 0 R
/Prev 9554 0 R
/Next 9548 0 R
>>
endobj
9556 0 obj
<<
/Title (The diagram shows ten entities. FLATS is the central property entity: one flat can have many residents, many visitors, and many maintenance bills. RESIDENTS is the central people entity: one resident can make many payments, raise many complaints, and make many amenity bookings. Each PAYMENT belongs to exactly one maintenance bill and one resident, and each AMENITY_BOOKING links one resident to one amenity. STAFF and NOTICES are independent entities that do not reference other tables.)
/Dest [36 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9549 0 R
/Next 9557 0 R
>>
